## Build systems, not willpower

- **Use reminders and alarms** for appointments, medicines and bills
- keep **keys, wallet and phone** in the same place every day
- use a **visual planner** or whiteboard
- break big tasks into **small, clear steps**
- try **body doubling**: working alongside someone else, in person or online

## Time

- use **timers** to see time passing
- add **extra time** to how long you think something will take
- set alarms to leave the house

## Money

Impulsive spending can be a problem. It can help to set up **direct debits** for bills, use **spending limits** in your banking app, and wait 24 hours before big purchases.

## Sleep, food and exercise

- a regular **sleep routine** helps concentration
- eat regular meals, especially if medicine reduces your appetite
- **exercise** can help with focus and mood

## Emotions

Many people with ADHD feel emotions intensely, or are very sensitive to criticism or rejection. Talking to others with ADHD, or to a therapist, can help.

## Play to your strengths

Many people with ADHD are creative, energetic and good in a crisis. Finding work and activities that suit how your brain works can make a big difference.

## Support

**ADHD UK**, the **ADHD Foundation** and local support groups offer information and community.
